rve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186415107 "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186415273 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(2,0)$ to $(3,-2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186415274 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\operatorname{arctan}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186415276 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=6xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186415277 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186415278 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x+y}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,3,1)$ to $(0,1,-1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$"] [17592186415279 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z-y.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,1)$ to $(2,-2,0)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$"] [17592186415280 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=y{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(\\pi ,1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186415312 "![](images/98/981d966645eec322afbfe28a5a1f519eeab8b3557692dcce368a87fe748dddd6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186415313 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186415314 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2y\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186415315 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186415770 "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186415773 "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=$"] [17592186415774 "![](images/b4/b4ce481ac8babb7a3e5707f1ef44417b12e73abe4b4c2143c19b45332642c6d2.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment  from the point $B(0,8)$ to the point $A(-2,8),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from the point $A(-2,8)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186415775 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186415866 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}-xy+11,{y}^{2}+1⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$"] [17592186416109 "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}?\n$$"] [17592186416110 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186416111 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\